<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sometimes I Feel Like a Motherless Child</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">era of slavery in the United States; sung by the Fisk Jubilee Singers in the 1870s</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Sometimes I Feel Like a Motherless Child&quot;, also sung as &quot;Motherless Child&quot;, is a traditional spiritual that goes back to the era of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. One early performance was by the Fisk Jubilee Singers in the 1870s. Samuel Coleridge-Taylor made it the first movement of his Trio in E minor in 1893, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-robeson">Paul Robeson</a> recorded it several times from 1926 onwards.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Sometimes I Feel Like a Motherless Child</b>&quot;, also sung as &quot;Motherless Child&quot;, is a traditional spiritual that goes back to the era of slavery in the United States.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sometimes-i-feel-like-a-motherless-child">Sometimes I Feel Like a Motherless Child</a></li></ul></section>
